<h2>How hostility actually unfolds</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. The departure.</strong> The child's deposition contradicts the FIR, the statement to police, or the statement recorded by the Magistrate under Section 183 BNSS (formerly Section 164 CrPC) — a total denial, or the more common partial dilution: the act shrinks, the identification blurs.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Leave to cross-examine.</strong> The prosecutor seeks the court's leave to put questions in the nature of cross-examination to its own witness, confronting the child with the earlier statements in the manner the evidence law prescribes.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. The contradiction record.</strong> Each prior statement is put, marked and proved through the recording officer — the mechanical step on which the entire later analysis depends. Contradictions not properly proved do not exist in law.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. The sifting.</strong> At judgment, the court evaluates the whole testimony with the rest of the record — accepting what is credible, discarding what is not, from both the examination-in-chief and the hostile phase.</div>
</div>

<h2>What earlier statements can and cannot do</h2>
<p>The architecture here decides most resiling cases. Statements to the police during investigation serve to contradict, not to convict. The statement recorded by a Magistrate stands on a higher footing of solemnity but remains, in the ordinary course, corroborative or contradictive material rather than substantive proof. What this means practically: a prosecution reduced to prior statements alone usually fails, while a prosecution that can pair the prior statements with independent anchors — medical findings consistent with the allegation, the testimony of the teacher or neighbour to whom the child first disclosed, call records, DNA — can survive even a fully hostile victim. The trial becomes a contest over those anchors.</p>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Why children resile:</strong> the accused is very often within the family or its economic orbit, and the pressures on the child between statement and deposition — reconciliation, marriage of the accused to a relative, financial dependence, simple exhaustion — are exactly why POCSO builds in support persons, in-camera trials, screened depositions and tight timelines. Every month between chargesheet and deposition is a month pressure can work; both the prosecution and the court control that calendar.</p>
</div>

<h2>The defence perspective, honestly stated</h2>
<p>Hostility helps the defence, but less automatically than assumed. A conviction can rest on the credible parts of a partly hostile witness's testimony, and over-cross-examining a resiled child — pushing past the denial into theatre — can resurrect sympathy and credibility the hostility had dissolved. The disciplined defence course is to ensure contradictions are formally proved, to test the surviving anchors (the medical evidence's actual consistency, the disclosure witness's opportunity), and to address the Section 29 presumption squarely by showing the foundational facts themselves are unproved. Where the resiling reflects a settlement, the defence should also be candid with the client about quashing: constitutional courts examine POCSO compromises with deep reservation, and an acquittal built on procured hostility is vulnerable for years.</p>

<h2>The prosecution and the court's toolkit</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Seek deposition dates early — Section 35 POCSO's timeline for recording the child's evidence exists precisely to beat the pressure window. Use the support person and vulnerable witness courtroom infrastructure; a child deposing in a screened, familiar setting resiles less. Where hostility appears procured, place the surrounding circumstances on record and invite the court's attention — courts can summon recording officers, examine the support person, and in appropriate cases direct action against those who tampered. And prosecutors should re-examine after the hostile cross: the power to clarify is routinely forgotten, and the clarified record is what the judgment will quote.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Note:</strong> this explainer discusses the legal framework in general terms; it is not legal advice. Child sexual abuse is a sensitive subject, and identities of victims are protected by law in all reporting and discussion.</p>
</div>
